  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Mississippi has $5.7B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state saw 82,107 awards with an average award size of $69.7K. Obligations are down 28.5% year over year. Obligations by fiscal year were: FY21 $4.9B, FY22 $5B, FY23 $8.7B, FY24 $6.5B, FY25 $8B, FY26 $5.7B. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer with $4.9B in obligations. Ship Building and Repairing is the largest industry with $3.7B in obligations. HUNTINGTON INGALLS INCORPORATED is the top vendor with $3.6B in obligations, followed by V2X AEROSPACE LLC with $434M and NOVA SPACE SOLUTIONS, LLC with $90.2M. The dataset for FY2026 to date is partial, and current-year totals reflect fiscal year to date activity. This snapshot shows concentrated buying in defense and shipbuilding, sizable award volume, and a notable year-over-year decline in obligations.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who buys the most federal contracts in Mississippi?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $4.9B. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $291M, the Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $140M, the Department of Agriculture obligated $79.4M, and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $68.5M. The Department of Justice obligated $65.9M and the Department of Commerce obligated $42.6M. The General Services Administration obligated $42.5M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which vendors and industries capture the largest shares?",
        "body": "HUNTINGTON INGALLS INCORPORATED obligated $3.6B. V2X AEROSPACE LLC obligated $434M and NOVA SPACE SOLUTIONS, LLC obligated $90.2M. Ship Building and Repairing accounted for $3.7B, Other Support Activities for Air Transportation accounted for $478M, and Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction accounted for $156M."
      }
    ]
  },
